\section{What it corresponds to in hBayesDM}{

Nothing, and that is worth stating plainly because every other family
in this package has a counterpart. hBayesDM ships \code{choiceRT_ddm},
which is this family's choice rule with NO learning: one drift rate
per subject and no value store. It also ships \code{bandit2arm_delta},
which is this family's learning rule with a softmax instead of a
diffusion. \code{rlddm()} is the two joined, and the join is the model
rather than a renaming of either.

Where the names do map, they map as hBayesDM's \code{choiceRT_ddm} spells
the diffusion: its \code{alpha} is the boundary separation, which is \code{bs}
here, its \code{beta} is the start point, which is \code{bias}, its \code{delta} is
the drift rate, which is \code{drift} times the value difference, and its
\code{tau} is the non-decision time, which is \code{ndt}. \code{alpha} here is the
LEARNING RATE, as it is in every other family in this package. That
collision is why the diffusion parameters carry \code{frmtmb.eam}'s names
rather than hBayesDM's: one convention across this package's families
is worth more than an inherited one that would make \code{alpha} mean two
different things two families apart.
}

\section{The data a trial carries}{

The response is the response TIME. The boundary reached travels in
\code{dec()}, coded 0 for the lower boundary and 1 for the upper, which is
the term and the coding \code{\link[frmtmb.eam:wiener]{frmtmb.eam::wiener()}} uses. Arm 1 is the
lower boundary and arm 2 the upper, so \code{reward(pay1, pay2)} is in the
same arm order as every other family here.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{frm(bf(rt | dec(choice) + reward(pay1, pay2) ~ 1 + (1 | id),
       drift ~ 1, bs ~ 1, ndt ~ 1, bias ~ 1),
    family = rlddm(subject = id, trial = trial), data = d)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Holding the start point at a half}{

Most of the reinforcement-learning literature fixes \code{bias} at 0.5,
because with two ARMS rather than a correct and an error response
there is no reason for the accumulator to start nearer one boundary.
It is an ordinary distributional parameter here, so it is estimated
by default and held with \code{bf()}'s constant form:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{frm(bf(rt | dec(choice) + reward(pay1, pay2) ~ 1 + (1 | id),
       drift ~ 1, bs ~ 1, ndt ~ 1, bias = 0.5), ...)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Estimating it is the more general model and costs one parameter;
fixing it is what makes \code{drift} and \code{bs} easier to separate on a
short session.
}

\section{The non-decision time is bounded, not logged}{

The density is zero at and below \code{ndt}, so the likelihood has a hard
edge at \code{ndt = min(rt)} and a log link would let the optimizer walk
over it. \code{ndt} therefore gets a logit scaled onto \verb{(0, max_ndt)},
with \code{max_ndt} defaulting to the fastest response in the data, which
makes the constraint structural. This is the same construction
\code{\link[frmtmb.eam:wiener]{frmtmb.eam::wiener()}} uses and it is written again here rather than
borrowed, because a link is not what that package exports.
}

\section{Recovery, measured}{

\code{dev/learn-recovery-round2.R} runs it and \code{?frmtmb.learn} carries the
table. All five parameters recover at 30 subjects by 100 trials with
a random intercept on the learning rate: every bias is inside its
Monte Carlo error, and 60 of 60 replicates produced a usable
interval. Coverage is at the nominal rate for \code{drift}, \code{bs}, \code{ndt}
and \code{bias}, and 0.87 for \code{alpha}, so a learning rate's Wald interval
is slightly optimistic here and should be read as such.

THE PAIR THAT TRADES OFF IS NOT THE ONE TO EXPECT, and this section
said otherwise before the study was run. A first-passage density is
driven largely by the RATIO of the drift to the boundary, so \code{drift}
and \code{bs} look like the pair at risk; measured, their estimates
correlate at 0.17 across replicates, which is nothing. What does
co-vary is \code{bs} with \code{ndt}, at -0.57, and \code{drift} with \code{bias}, at
+0.56. Both make sense after the fact: the boundary separation and
the non-decision time are the two ways to make responses slower, and
the drift and the start point are the two ways to favor a boundary.
Read those two pairs together.
}

\section{Drawing data needs RWiener, and fitting does not}{

\code{\link[=frm_task_simulate]{frm_task_simulate()}} on this family draws a boundary and a time
JOINTLY from the diffusion, through
\code{\link[frmtmb.eam:ddm_simulate]{frmtmb.eam::ddm_simulate()}}, and that route is exact only when
\code{RWiener} is installed. Without it the draw is refused rather than
approximated: \code{frmtmb.eam}'s own fallback draws the boundary first
and then rejects paths until one reaches it, which fails outright on
the rows where one boundary is strongly favored, and a simulator
that works on most rows and errors on the rest is worse than one
that says what it needs. Nothing about FITTING needs \code{RWiener}; the
likelihood is \code{\link[frmtmb.eam:wiener_lpdf]{frmtmb.eam::wiener_lpdf()}} and is exact either way.
}

\section{Where the density comes from}{

\code{\link[frmtmb.eam:wiener_lpdf]{frmtmb.eam::wiener_lpdf()}}, through the export rather than through a
colon. The Navarro and Fuss (2009) series pair, its smooth blend and
its tape safety all belong to that package and are not repeated here.
This is the only dependency one extension of frmtmb has on another,
and it is one function.
}

